    {
      "cve": {
        "id": "CVE-2026-115560",
        "sourceIdentifier": "security@php.net",
        "published": "2026-11-19T15:17:24.610",
        "lastModified": "2026-11-22T01:16:52.303",
        "vulnStatus": "Awaiting Analysis",
        "cveTags": [],
        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en",
            "value": "Session serializer mismatch can lead to remote code execution.\n\nSession upload progress, when enabled, can store attacker-controlled data in a session key using the php_binary or php_serialize session serializer. If the same session is later read with the php serializer, because session.serialize_handler is changed at runtime or differs between applications sharing a session store, bytes inside the key can be passed to the unserializer. A remote attacker able to submit a multipart upload can inject objects, which may lead to code execution when the reading application contains a suitable gadget chain, or to session corruption or denial of service.\n\nThis issue affects PHP versions before 8.2.36, 8.3.* before 8.3.37, 8.4.* before 8.4.28 and 8.5.* before 8.5.13."
          }
        ],
